Fresnel-Chug
Ingredients: GARAGE ROCK + 432Hz RELAXING MEDITATIVE STYLES + French Horn
Creative Catalyst: Lonely lighthouse keepers on sub-Arctic skerries broadcast fuzzed-out tape jams over maritime longwave radio to soothe passing freighters through polar nights.

Fresnel-Chug slows the jagged, three-chord aggression of vintage garage rock down to a therapeutic, rhythmic crawl, anchoring blown-out tube amps in deep 432Hz droning tunings. Cutting through the fuzz is the noble, melancholic timber of the French horn, functioning simultaneously as navigational foghorn and oceanic mantric drone, yielding music that feels simultaneously earth-shaking and tranquilizing.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 72–84 BPM. A languid, heavy-footed garage stomp operating in half-time; unquantized kick-and-tambourine patterns that mimic the rhythmic rise and fall of northern groundswells.
- Harmonic Palette: Modal drones built on D Dorian and G Mixolydian, tuned strictly to A=432 Hz pure intonation; open fifths and fuzz-saturated baritone power chords voiced deliberately to eliminate aggressive dissonances while maximizing chest-rattling physical resonance.
- Production Signatures: French horn tracked through a dual-speed Leslie rotary speaker cabinet for an undulating acoustic vibrato; garage guitar fuzz passed through oil-can delay units to soften high-frequency bite into pillowy velvet; Nagra reel-to-reel master tapes run at slightly slack tape tension to introduce gentle, slow wow-and-flutter.
- Dynamics: Cyclical swell-and-drain dynamics where stripped horn drones accumulate crunching guitar feedback and heavy floor-tom thuds, blooming into warm garage crescendos that breathe without jarring the listener's nervous system.

Scene & Origin Lore
The sound crystallized between 1974 and 1981 across the storm-battered lighthouses of Scotland's Outer Hebrides and Norway's Lofoten archipelago. Small crews of lighthouse keepers, marooned in total darkness for four months at a time, found that standard garage 45s induced claustrophobia, while traditional meditation discs felt toothless against raging gales. Combining beat-up pawnshop fuzz pedals, tape decks, and brass band French horns found in local chapel basements, they down-tuned their gear to A=432 Hz to calm their own racing heartbeats, beaming the resulting half-speed jam sessions out on longwave maritime emergency frequencies (2182 kHz). Merchant marine crews navigating treacherous shoals began actively seeking these unofficial pirate broadcasts to steady their nerves. Soon, an insular coastal subculture bloomed on mainland harbors: oil-tanned youth in grease-stained Guernsey wool and salt-cured dungarees gathered inside empty fish-salting warehouses, lying silently on mounds of dry netting while three-piece ensembles ground out meditative garage squall beneath oscillating amber lantern rigs.

Typical Instruments
- Lead & Brass Drone: Double French Horn (Conn 8D or Alexander 103), played open-bell and with stopping mutes through rotary speaker cabinets.
- Fuzz & Rhythm: 1960s Teisco or Silvertone electric guitars running into vintage germanium fuzz boxes and tube combos; Danelectro baritone guitars.
- Percussion & Texture: 3-piece vintage drum kit with tea-towel damped snares, oversized bass drums, brass shakers, and harmonium foot-bellows.
- Signal Processors: Watkins Copicat tape delays, oil-can echo boxes, and 432 Hz strobe tuners.

For Fans Of: Wooden Shjips, Colin Stetson, The Black Angels, Popol Vuh

