Track 3: Brass Bells Devour the Skerry
- Story & Context: Long after the complete automation of the Monach Isles lighthouse left its granite towers dark and deserted, North Sea navigators continued to report inexplicable low-frequency tones shuddering through their hulls whenever polar fog crippled their radar displays. When Captain Jenssen’s navigation screens died during the solstice storm of 1989, he cut the cargo freighter's diesel engines and steered solely toward a deep, repeating horn refrain and half-speed garage floor-tom thud drifting over the shoals. Coast Guard salvagers later boarding the island's drowned basement battery room discovered only salt-rusted spring-reverb tanks and the phantom refrain still inscribed upon the logbook wall: "we are sleeping under twenty fathoms of fuzz."
